From c55da0337f67616ee7be4bfb06b73fdd71ef7f91 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Abdu Benayad <abdu.benayad@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 03:06:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] epaint: shape right-to-left text in bidi runs and place
 rows in visual order

Text with right-to-left characters is now laid out per UAX #9:

* `layout` resolves bidi embedding levels once per job (`unicode-bidi`),
  skipped entirely for ASCII text and for text with no RTL character.
* `segment_into_runs` also splits where the level changes, and `shape_text`
  sets the harfrust direction from the level instead of guessing it, so
  digits inside Arabic or Hebrew shape left-to-right and read `123`.
* `layout_shaped_run` walks a right-to-left run's clusters backwards, so the
  glyph vector stays in logical order with increasing x. This restores the
  `glyphs.len() == char_count` invariant for RTL text (a 13-char Arabic
  string produced 20 glyphs before, which left the `TextEdit` caret in
  phantom slots past the end of the string) and keeps line breaking unchanged.
* After line breaking, `reorder_row_visually` applies rule L2 per row by
  rewriting x positions only; a glyph's index is still its char index.
* `Glyph` carries its `bidi_level`; `Row::char_at` and `Row::x_offset` use it
  to put the caret on the correct side of a glyph, and the background,
  underline and selection painters walk glyphs by x.

Paragraph direction is taken from the first strong character (P2/P3),
as `dir="auto"` does in a browser. No new `LayoutJob` field.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
